---
description: Apply when asked how long, how big, or how much effort something will take, or when scoping work
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---

- An estimate is a distribution; the honest product is a range plus the assumptions that would break it. The gut number is the optimistic bound, not the middle (the planning fallacy is the default, not a flaw).
- Pin "done" before sizing — fuzzy scope gets per-interpretation numbers ("CSV only: X; full: Y"); the gap forces the scope decision now. Include the whole cost: review, tests, docs, deploy, migration, coordination.
- Decompose into parts that resemble work actually done before and size by reference ("the last three similar endpoints took ~2 days each"). Mark each part known / variable / unknown — the unknowns are where estimates die.
- Attack unknowns with timeboxed spikes (read the docs, prototype the join, measure the volume) — an hour often collapses one to a known. Still unknown → estimate the discovery ("2 days to determine feasibility, then re-estimate"), never a number-shaped shrug.
- Integration and iteration are line items; a deadline is not an estimate — a fixed date converts to scope ("by the 15th: X and Y, not Z"). Deliver: `Likely N–M · assumes: <breakers> · biggest risk: <the unknown + its spike> · cut line: <what drops>`. Forced to one number → attach confidence.
- Never anchor on the number in the question, estimate under social pressure ("range in an hour" is professional), pad silently, do precision theater, or skip comparing estimates to actuals afterward — that loop is where calibration comes from.
